# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene before the parable. Matthew 19 opens with Jesus leaving Galilee and coming into the coasts of Judaea beyond Jordan, where Pharisees put a question to Him about divorce (19:1–3). Then “one came and said unto him, Good Master, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?” (19:16). Jesus answers with the commandments and then says, “If thou wilt be perfect, go and sell that thou hast, and give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in heaven: and come and follow me” (19:21). Matthew records that “when the young man heard that saying, he went away sorrowful: for he had great possessions” (19:22).'))
A(('p', '2.2 The word to the disciples. Jesus then speaks to His disciples: “a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven” (19:23). They are “exceedingly amazed, saying, Who then can be saved?” (19:25). Jesus answers, “With men this is impossible; but with God all things are possible” (19:26). This study reports those words as Matthew gives them and builds nothing further on them.'))
A(('p', '2.3 Peter’s question and the answer. “Then answered Peter and said unto him, Behold, we have forsaken all, and followed thee; what shall we have therefore?” (19:27). Jesus answers in two parts. To the Twelve He says, “ye which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of man shall sit in the throne of his glory, ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel” (19:28). Then He speaks of “every one that hath forsaken houses, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my name’s sake” (19:29). He closes with a saying: “But many that are first shall be last; and the last shall be first” (19:30).'))
A(('call', 'The Question That Stands Behind This Parable',
   'Peter asks, “what shall we have therefore?” (19:27). Jesus answers him at 19:28–30, and the parable follows without a break in Matthew’s text. Matthew does not say that the parable is Jesus’ further answer to Peter, and this study adds nothing to what he records.'))
A(('p', '2.4 How the parable begins. The parable opens with the word “For”: “For the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder” (20:1). Matthew does not mark a new scene, a new audience or a new day between 19:30 and 20:1, and he does not say what the word “For” joins. The parable ends with a saying that reverses the order of the words at 19:30: “So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen” (20:16). Immediately after it Matthew writes, “And Jesus going up to Jerusalem took the twelve disciples apart in the way” (20:17).'))
A(('p', '2.5 The prophetic context. Matthew has said what Jesus preached: “From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). He sent the Twelve with a limited charge: “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:5–6). Jesus’ answer to Peter speaks of “the twelve tribes of Israel” (19:28). The parable uses the same words, “the kingdom of heaven” (20:1).'))
