# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The opening comparison (20:1). “For the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard.” The comparison is to the kingdom of heaven, “like unto a man that is an householder.” The householder goes out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The first hiring (20:2). “And when he had agreed with the labourers for a penny a day, he sent them into his vineyard.” The householder agrees with these labourers for a penny a day and sends them into the vineyard. The text does not say how many were hired.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The third hour (20:3–4). “And he went out about the third hour, and saw others standing idle in the marketplace, And said unto them; Go ye also into the vineyard, and whatsoever is right I will give you. And they went their way.” This time no sum is named. The promise is “whatsoever is right I will give you,” and “they went their way.”'))
A(('p', '3.4 The sixth and ninth hours (20:5). “Again he went out about the sixth and ninth hour, and did likewise.” The text says only that he “did likewise.” It records no words and no number for these hirings.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The eleventh hour (20:6–7). “And about the eleventh hour he went out, and found others standing idle, and saith unto them, Why stand ye here all the day idle? They say unto him, Because no man hath hired us. He saith unto them, Go ye also into the vineyard; and whatsoever is right, that shall ye receive.” Here the householder asks a question and receives an answer: “no man hath hired us.” He sends them in with the same promise as before.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The reckoning begins (20:8). “So when even was come, the lord of the vineyard saith unto his steward, Call the labourers, and give them their hire, beginning from the last unto the first.” The man who was called “householder” is now called “the lord of the vineyard.” He speaks to “his steward,” and the order of payment is from the last to the first.'))
A(('p', '3.7 The payment (20:9–10). “And when they came that were hired about the eleventh hour, they received every man a penny. But when the first came, they supposed that they should have received more; and they likewise received every man a penny.” Those hired at the eleventh hour receive a penny each. The first suppose they will receive more, and they receive a penny each.'))
A(('p', '3.8 The murmuring (20:11–12). “And when they had received it, they murmured against the goodman of the house, Saying, These last have wrought but one hour, and thou hast made them equal unto us, which have borne the burden and heat of the day.” The first murmur. Their words have three parts: the last worked one hour, they have been made equal, and the first bore the burden and heat of the day.'))
A(('p', '3.9 The answer (20:13–15). “But he answered one of them, and said, Friend, I do thee no wrong: didst not thou agree with me for a penny? Take that thine is, and go thy way: I will give unto this last, even as unto thee. Is it not lawful for me to do what I will with mine own? Is thine eye evil, because I am good?” He answers one of them. He names the agreement for a penny, tells him to take what is his and go, says he will give to the last as to him, and asks two questions.'))
A(('p', '3.10 The closing saying (20:16). “So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen.” The saying begins with “So.” It comes directly after the answer and is not itself a scene. It names the last and the first, and it ends with the many who are called and the few who are chosen.'))
