A(('p', '3.11 What the story contains. The text names a householder, a vineyard, labourers, a penny a day, five times of going out (early morning, third, sixth, ninth and eleventh hours), a marketplace, idle men, a steward, an evening, a payment from last to first, a murmuring, and a friend who is answered. The action moves from hiring, to a reckoning, to a payment, to a complaint, to an answer.'))
A(('p', '3.12 What the story leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the householder is. Jesus does not say who the labourers of any hour are. Jesus does not say what the vineyard is, or what the hours are, or what the penny is. Jesus does not say who the steward is. Jesus does not say who “the first” and “the last” are in the story, or who the one answered is. Jesus does not say what the eye that is evil is. The story gives men hired at five times of the day, a penny to each, a complaint, and an answer, and it stops at the closing saying.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. Matthew records no explanation of the parable and no request for one. After the closing saying Matthew turns to Jesus “going up to Jerusalem” (20:17). This section therefore records no identification of any part of the story, because there is none to record. It records the two sayings Jesus speaks and where each stands.'))
A(('p', '4.2 The saying before the parable. At 19:30 Jesus says, “But many that are first shall be last; and the last shall be first.” It is spoken as the close of His answer to Peter, before the parable begins. It names the first and the last. It does not say who they are.'))
A(('p', '4.3 The saying after the parable. At 20:16 Jesus says, “So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen.” It names the last and the first, in the opposite order to 19:30, and it names the many who are called and the few who are chosen. It does not say who the many are, who the few are, who calls, who chooses, or which part of the story it draws on. Both sayings are recorded here as sayings and not as identifications.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not identify. The parable names many parts of the story and leaves each of them without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the householder or the lord of the vineyard. Jesus does not identify the vineyard. Jesus does not identify the labourers of the morning, of the third hour, of the sixth and ninth hours, or of the eleventh hour. Jesus does not identify the hours. Jesus does not identify the penny. Jesus does not identify the steward. Jesus does not identify the first or the last. Jesus does not identify the one who is answered. Jesus does not identify the eye that is evil. Jesus does not identify the called or the chosen.'))
