A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. This parable follows the answer to Peter on the way through Judaea (19:1; 20:17). The Two Sons and the Wicked Tenants are told in the temple after the question about authority (21:23). The Talents belongs to the Olivet teaching (24:3; 25:14). The Unforgiving Servant is told in answer to Peter’s question about forgiving a brother (18:21). A shared word such as “vineyard,” “householder” or “servants” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The parable has a householder, who is also the lord of the vineyard, his steward, and labourers hired at five times of the day. The Wicked Tenants has a householder, husbandmen, servants and a son. The Two Sons has a man and two sons. The Talents has a man who travels, servants and a lord who returns. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The laborers moves from a hiring, to a reckoning, to a payment, to a murmuring, to an answer. The Wicked Tenants moves from a planting, to servants sent, to a son sent and killed, to a question about what the lord will do. The Talents moves from a giving, to a going away, to a return and an account. None of those movements is read into the other.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the penny, the hours and the order of payment belong to Matthew 20 only. Second, the sayings of 19:30 and 20:16 are Jesus’ words in this setting, and the nearly identical words at 22:14 and in Luke 13:30 are not read into them. Third, the vineyard here is not read as the vineyard of Matthew 21 or of any other passage.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (10:5–6). The parable belongs to that ministry. The Kingdom it speaks of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel, and Matthew places the parable in an answer that speaks of the Twelve and “the twelve tribes of Israel” (19:28).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The kingdom of heaven that Jesus compares to a householder who hired labourers into his vineyard is the Kingdom He announced during His earthly ministry. It is not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The householder, the vineyard, the hours, the penny and the steward are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The parable is not read as a universal picture of how any person is saved or rewarded. The hiring, the going, the working and the receiving of a penny are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson against murmuring.** The first murmured against the goodman of the house (20:11). A reader learns to notice when his own complaint rests on comparing what another received.',
    '**A lesson of keeping to what was agreed.** The householder says, “didst not thou agree with me for a penny?” (20:13). A reader learns to weigh his own claim against the word that was actually given.',
    '**A lesson about the eye.** The householder asks, “Is thine eye evil, because I am good?” (20:15). A reader learns to ask whether another’s goodness has become the occasion for his own envy.',
    '**A lesson of humility.** The last are first and the first last (20:16). A reader learns not to presume a place from the order in which he came.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom’s vineyard and payment are not turned into the Body’s hope, and the householder’s answer is not turned into a rule for the Body’s reward. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'The kingdom of heaven is like a householder who went out early in the morning, and again at the third, sixth, ninth and eleventh hours, to hire labourers into his vineyard. At evening his steward called them and paid each a penny, beginning from the last. The first supposed they should have received more, and they murmured. The householder answered one of them with the agreement for a penny, his right to do what he will with his own, and the question, “Is thine eye evil, because I am good?” Jesus ends with “So the last shall be first, and the first last: for many be called, but few chosen,” and before the parable He had said, “But many that are first shall be last; and the last shall be first.” He gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that the householder seeks labourers all day, keeps his word, does what he will with his own, is good, and reverses the order of first and last. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the householder, the labourers, the hours, the penny or the vineyard are, or that makes working or receiving a penny a way of being saved, goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a householder hires at the morning, the third, sixth, ninth and eleventh hours; at evening each is paid a penny from the last to the first; the first murmur; the householder answers; the last shall be first, and the first last.',
    '**The meaning:** the householder seeks labourers all day; he keeps his word; he does what he will with his own; he is good; the order of first and last is reversed.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the householder, the labourers, the hours, the penny, the steward or the vineyard; nothing carried in from the parables beside it.',
    '**The present lesson:** no murmuring, keeping to what was agreed, no envy of another’s goodness, and humility, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

