A(('bul', [
    '**Between characters.** The householder, the steward, the labourers of five times of the day and the one who is answered are distinct, and Jesus names none of them.',
    '**Between actions.** The householder goes out five times, agrees with the first, promises “whatsoever is right” to the rest, calls for payment from the last to the first, and answers one who murmurs. The labourers go into the vineyard, receive a penny, and the first murmur.',
    '**Between settings.** The parable stands in Matthew 20, after the answer to Peter and before the going up to Jerusalem. The Wicked Tenants and the Two Sons stand in the temple. The Talents stands in the Olivet teaching.',
    '**Between this parable and its neighbors.** The penny, the hours and the payment from the last to the first belong to this parable only. The son who is killed belongs to the Wicked Tenants. The returning lord and the account belong to the Talents.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the householder, the vineyard, the labourers of any hour, the penny, the steward, the first or the last. The Body of Christ is none of them.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from the Wicked Tenants, the Two Sons, the Talents, the Wedding Banquet, or Luke 13:30, which uses nearly the same words.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Hiring, working and receiving a penny are actions in a story. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

